3720. Lexicographically Smallest Permutation Greater Than Target (Medium)
 


Hint

You are given two strings s and target, both having length n, consisting of lowercase English letters.

Return the lexicographically smallest of s that is strictly greater than target. If no permutation of s is lexicographically strictly greater than target, return an empty string.

A string a is lexicographically strictly greater than a string b (of the same length) if in the first position where a and b differ, string a has a letter that appears later in the alphabet than the corresponding letter in b.

 

Example 1:

Input: s = "abc", target = "bba"

Output: "bca"

Explanation:

    The permutations of s (in lexicographical order) are "abc", "acb", "bac", "bca", "cab", and "cba".
    The lexicographically smallest permutation that is strictly greater than target is "bca".

Example 2:

Input: s = "leet", target = "code"

Output: "eelt"

Explanation:

    The permutations of s (in lexicographical order) are "eelt", "eetl", "elet", "elte", "etel", "etle", "leet", "lete", "ltee", "teel", "tele", and "tlee".
    The lexicographically smallest permutation that is strictly greater than target is "eelt".

Example 3:

Input: s = "baba", target = "bbaa"

Output: ""

Explanation:

    The permutations of s (in lexicographical order) are "aabb", "abab", "abba", "baab", "baba", and "bbaa".
    None of them is lexicographically strictly greater than target. Therefore, the answer is "".

 

Constraints:

    1 <= s.length == target.length <= 300
    s and target consist of only lowercase English letters.



Test Case:
Input
s =
"abc"
target =
"bba"
Output
"bca"


Answer:
class Solution {
    public String lexGreaterPermutation(String s, String target) {
        int n = s.length();

        int[] count = new int[26];

        // Count characters in s
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            count[c - 'a']++;
        }

        StringBuilder prefix = new StringBuilder();

        // Match target from left to right
        int i = 0;

        while (i < n) {
            int ch = target.charAt(i) - 'a';

            if (count[ch] == 0) {
                break;
            }

            count[ch]--;
            prefix.append(target.charAt(i));
            i++;
        }

        /*
         * We currently matched target[0 ... i-1].
         *
         * First try to make the string greater at position i.
         * If that is impossible, backtrack to previous positions.
         */
        for (int pos = i; pos >= 0; pos--) {

            // If pos < i, restore the character that was
            // previously matched at this position.
            if (pos < i) {
                int restored = target.charAt(pos) - 'a';
                count[restored]++;
                prefix.deleteCharAt(prefix.length() - 1);
            }

            // If pos == n, there is no position to increase.
            if (pos == n) {
                continue;
            }

            int targetChar = target.charAt(pos) - 'a';

            // Find the smallest available character
            // greater than target[pos].
            for (int c = targetChar + 1; c < 26; c++) {

                if (count[c] > 0) {

                    StringBuilder ans = new StringBuilder(prefix);

                    // Put the larger character
                    ans.append((char) ('a' + c));
                    count[c]--;

                    // Put all remaining characters in sorted order
                    for (int x = 0; x < 26; x++) {
                        while (count[x] > 0) {
                            ans.append((char) ('a' + x));
                            count[x]--;
                        }
                    }

                    return ans.toString();
                }
            }
        }

        return "";
    }
}

Complexity: O(n × 26) time and O(n) space.



Explanation:
Approach

We need the smallest permutation of s that is strictly greater than target.

The key idea is similar to finding the next permutation, but target itself may not be a permutation of s.

Count how many times each character occurs in s.
Try to match target from left to right.
As long as we can use the exact character from target, keep matching.
At some position i, if we cannot match target[i], we need to:
go back to some previous position,
choose the smallest available character greater than target[i],
then put all remaining characters in sorted order.
This gives the lexicographically smallest answer.
If we cannot make the string greater at any position, return "".

For example:

s = "abc"
target = "bba"

We try to follow bba:

target: b b a

From s = abc, we can use b:

prefix = "b"
remaining = a,c

Next target character is b, but there is no b left.

So we backtrack and try to make the previous position bigger than b.

The smallest available character greater than b is c.

Then put the remaining characters in sorted order:

"b" + "c" + "a"
= "bca"

Therefore:

"bca"

is the answer.
